Directory operations, and streams of files.

Reading one file was section eight; this section is about the directory — listing what's in it, walking a whole tree, and finding files by pattern. And the elegant part is that files exposes all of these as streams of path — so the entire module-seven toolkit, filter, map, sorted, count, collect, applies directly to the filesystem. Walking a directory tree becomes a pipeline.

Start with files-dot-list — one directory, one level deep. Files-dot-list, of a directory, returns a stream of path, of the immediate entries of that directory — not recursive. And like files-dot-lines, it holds an operating-system resource — an open directory handle — so it's auto-closeable, and it must be used in a try-with-resources. So: try, files-dot-list of dir, and then over the entries, filter to the regular files, skipping sub-directories, map each to its file name, sort them, and print each. And the directory handle is released at the end of the block.

Next, files-dot-walk — the whole tree, recursively. Files-dot-walk, of a directory, returns a stream of path, of every entry beneath that directory, descending into sub-directories, depth-first. It's the same pipeline shape, now over an entire tree — and it needs the same non-negotiable try-with-resources, because a deep walk holds handles open as it goes. So: try, files-dot-walk of start, then filter the paths that end in dot-java, and count them. You can pass a depth limit — files-dot-walk of start and two — to bound how far down it descends. This one stream replaces the classic hand-written recursive directory-walk method, entirely.

Then there's files-dot-find — a walk with a built-in filter. When you're walking in order to match a predicate, files-dot-find fuses the walk and the filter. You give it a max depth, and a bi-predicate of path and basic-file-attributes — so the test can use cheap, already-fetched attributes, like size, modified time, and is-directory, without a separate stat call per file. So files-dot-find, of start, and max-depth, with a test: the path is a regular file, and its size is over a million bytes. That finds every file over a megabyte, anywhere in the tree.

Now, the one rule you cannot forget. Every directory stream — list, walk, and find — is backed by an open filesystem resource, unlike an in-memory list-dot-stream. Forget the try-with-resources, and you leak directory handles. Do enough of it, and the process runs out — and every further file operation fails. So the discipline from section six is not optional here: a files stream is always auto-closeable; always open it in a try-with-resources. In-memory collection streams, from module seven, don't need this — only the ones backed by an operating-system resource do. And the tell is that the stream source is a file, or a directory.

Why is this design so satisfying? Notice what's happened across sections seven, eight, and nine. The filesystem — directories, trees, huge files — has been re-expressed as streams of path. So a single, uniform vocabulary — filter, map, count, collect — queries a list, a file's lines, and an entire directory tree, all alike. "Find every dot-java file over a megabyte, modified this week" is one files-dot-find pipeline — not a recursive method with manual bookkeeping. Module seven's abstraction, and module eight's input-output, meet right here. And the result reads like the question you're asking.
